---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const lang = 'en';
---

<BaseLayout
  lang={lang}
  title="About"
  description="Ike, the person behind Xeno Labs — background in operations management, IT support and systems administration, now building AI agents and automation from between Australia and Mexico."
>
  <section class="container-page py-20 sm:py-28">
    <div class="grid gap-12 sm:grid-cols-[1.1fr,0.9fr] sm:gap-16">
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-accent-light">About</p>
        <h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl font-medium tracking-tight text-fg sm:text-5xl">Hi, I'm Ike.</h1>

        <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-fg/60">
          <p>
            I'm Australian, currently based between Australia and Mexico. My path here
            wasn't a straight line — ten years in operations management, followed by
            more recent years in IT support and systems administration, both for
            Australian firms working remotely in wealth strategy and finance. It's the
            kind of work that has you fixing the small, unglamorous things that quietly
            keep a business running: processes, accounts, software, the odd fire that
            needs putting out before anyone else notices it started.
          </p>
          <p>
            Somewhere along the way I started noticing the same pattern everywhere: a lot
            of the work people were doing by hand didn't actually need a human doing it
            by hand. It needed something that could follow instructions reliably, at any
            hour, without getting bored halfway through. That's what pulled me toward
            building with AI — not as a buzzword, but as a genuinely useful way to give
            people their time back.
          </p>
          <p>
            These days Xeno Labs is where that shows up: custom AI agents, small internal
            tools, and integrations built for the way a specific team actually works,
            rather than a one-size-fits-all product they have to bend themselves around.
          </p>
          <p>
            I like building things end to end — I've shipped everything from a
            project-management app to a voice assistant you can talk to while driving,
            usually because the tool I wanted didn't quite exist yet. If that sounds like
            your situation too, I'd like to hear about it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-fg/10 bg-surface p-6">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-fg/40">Background</h2>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-fg/60">
            <li>10 years in operations management</li>
            <li>5+ years in IT support & systems administration</li>
            <li>Now focused on AI agents, automation & internal tools</li>
            <li>Based between Australia and Mexico — working with clients remotely</li>
            <li>Australian, bilingual (English / Spanish)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-fg/10 bg-surface p-6">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-fg/40">How I work</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-fg/60">
            Direct, low-fuss communication. I'd rather tell you a project isn't worth
            doing than build something you don't need. If it's not going to save you
            real time, I'll say so.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
